WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.695
Many integer defects appear when a value crosses a type boundary.

2
00:00:03.695 --> 00:00:12.430
C++ has well-defined rules for promotions and conversions, but those rules are not always the same as the business rule or safety rule the code

3
00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:12.766
intended.

4
00:00:12.766 --> 00:00:16.461
A value can compile cleanly while changing meaning at the boundary.

5
00:00:16.461 --> 00:00:18.141
Implicit conversions deserve special attention.

6
00:00:18.141 --> 00:00:20.828
A smaller type can be promoted before arithmetic.

7
00:00:20.828 --> 00:00:24.187
A signed value can be compared with an unsigned value.

8
00:00:24.187 --> 00:00:27.211
A result can be assigned into a narrower type.

9
00:00:27.211 --> 00:00:34.601
These operations can be legal C++, but still surprising to a reviewer who is reading for domain intent rather than language mechanics.

10
00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:37.961
Signed and unsigned comparisons are a common source of confusion.

11
00:00:37.961 --> 00:00:44.008
A guard that seems to reject negative values may not behave as expected if conversion occurs before comparison.

12
00:00:44.008 --> 00:00:51.734
A loop counter, container size, or sentinel value can become difficult to reason about when signedness changes from one expression to the next.

13
00:00:51.734 --> 00:00:54.086
Narrowing conversions should be explicit and justified.

14
00:00:54.086 --> 00:01:02.484
Before converting a large integer into a smaller type, or a signed integer into an unsigned type, the code should check that the destination can

15
00:01:02.484 --> 00:01:06.515
represent the value and that the value is valid for the domain.

16
00:01:06.515 --> 00:01:09.875
A successful cast does not prove the conversion was safe.

17
00:01:09.875 --> 00:01:15.922
Teams can reduce mistakes by using conversion helpers, strong typedefs, wrapper functions, or project-standard checked conversion utilities.

18
00:01:15.922 --> 00:01:18.945
The goal is not ceremony for its own sake.

19
00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:24.320
The goal is to make boundary-crossing decisions visible, testable, and easy to search during review.
